I was sent the script for the  kindergarten teacher and a you know a  pretty conventional way you know they  they just approached me about it and I  had a sort of a rare reaction I've had  it before but we're we're really  contained inside the script was I think  the possibility of telling a really  complicated interesting story sometimes  you know I'll I'll be approached about  something and it won't be in the script  it'll be sort of partially in the script  but oh you know Robert Duvall and Jeff  Bridges are in it or you know whatever  it is in this case I didn't know Sarah  Colangelo at the time but she had  written something which I just knew when  I finished it I was like I need to do  this I have a lot of thoughts about  preparation  you know I it shifts and it's changed a  lot as I've as I've changed and shifted  you know I think I think there came a  time when I was maybe like 35 36 and a  few years ago where I really understood  how to work I would just break down a  scene into what is the event of the  scene and what do I need and what are my  obstacles you know kind of classic way  of looking at scene and in order to  answer the questions I would I don't  know I kind of used like a like a dreamy  almost a kind of unconscious part of my  mind in terms of thinking about yeah how  to break the scene down and then once I  would get on set I would have some kind  of point of view about what I wanted to  seem to be but I would hopefully usually  just let it go and see where it took me  but I would be able to know okay you  know I even though the scene is written  this way and these are the lines and it  isn't explicit I think this scene is an  apology for example and so I'm gonna I'm  gonna play it that way and even though  I'm saying these words to you I'm gonna  be apologizing you know for example and  then lately I've been like throwing it  away even more it's not that I don't  believe in work and doing the technical  work I do and I think it's really served  me and  I think since I've started really taking  that seriously my work has vastly  improved but I'm also now kind of toying  with like I don't know I don't know how  to like really put my finger on it like  just sort of going at it all from a sort  of Trippier place like more like a  dreamier place a more kind of  unconscious place just opening my mind  my my heart to the ideas that I think  are in the piece and seeing what ends up  bubbling up as well as doing the other  work I think but I think mostly with the  kindergarten teacher I don't think I  knew that this is what I was doing at  the time I don't know what I was doing I  was doing the like just open myself up  to the project kind of thing I was just  very difficult to describe but I think  what I was doing was getting in touch  with the part of myself that can that I  think many women are are getting in  touch with right now the part of myself  that was like there's a cost and there's  a consequence to having twisted myself  into pretzels and bent over backwards to  fit myself into the culture that we live  in as a woman and yeah getting in touch  in some way with what that cost is  because that's what I really think the  kindergarten teacher is about there are  some very simple things that you just  have to do almost directorial II our  movie hinges on the audience having to  decide for themselves at each moment  whether they understand and empathize  with her or are repelled by her and it's  got a shift and change all the way  through the movie I mean to the very  last moment and so I mean there's some  things you just you just know you have  to think through like in order for that  to work the movie has to start with her  being warm curious friendly really great  kindergarten teacher you know there's a  way to play it I guess where she's like  a dark sad shadow and there's maybe  that's even a more conventional way  to read the script and begin it I think  that would have alienated everybody I  think she has to be somebody who could  be your friend it could be my friend you  know  so that's that's one thing in terms of  acting with the little boy parker who  plays Jimmy you know I have a lot of  strong feelings about this I I don't  personally believe that a five-year-old  is an actor I just don't think it's  possible and I don't think it's fair to  ask them to do it and we have this weird  job where five-year-olds have to come to  work and I have a five I had if I wrote  the time I was six year old now but I  had a child exactly the same age as  Parker so I was really in touch with I  mean how truly vulnerable a  five-year-old is and I mean it's hard to  remember it's hard I don't remember now  cuz I have a six-year-old you know I  mean it's like but it's extreme and it's  it's something that's very different  than a grown-up and so there was in fact  a moment where even though I was so  deeply compelled by the script and the  ideas in it I almost didn't do it  because I thought how we can protect him  I don't want this child for one minute  to feel that his boundaries are crossed  in the way that the characters  boundaries are crossed and yet you know  the movie was made by a group of mothers  of small children I mean all of us Sarah  me the producers we all were really in  touch with that and so basically it was  very important to me I just thought  anyway even if it hadn't been like sort  of upsetting well not sort of an  upsetting story which it is I still  think the only way to work with a child  that age is super technically you cannot  ask him like you know can you go a  little deeper into the pain in your  heart I mean like five girls never even  understand us so we just it was more  like stand here and when I clap my hands  turn around and look at this X on the  wall or even you know no X on the wall  just Parker you know turns around and  looks at you and then say I don't want  to go you know and he turned around and  say  I don't want to go and we'd think nono  sing it like a song I don't want to go I  don't want to go you know and like just  imitate us just like a song and we had  we got this like um shorthand with him  where we would sing it and he would sing  it back to us and well I think he's so  bright I think what's so wonderful about  Parker is that he isn't performing  himself in the way that we all end up  doing and it's shocking to see how early  little kids even started to do it he's  not and he's really bright and smart but  I will say that it's Sarah who put  together that performance because if we  had done it any other way  we would have been compromising him and  we didn't compromise him I'm proud of a  lot of things I really am proud of it I  love sitting and I've seen the movie  twice all the way through with an  audience and then I've seen the end like  the sort of last sequence many times  because we've been doing a lot of q and  a's afterwards and i've never been  involved with a movie where the audience  is so vocal where they laugh and gasp  and like almost moan as they're watching  the movie at first I found that really  difficult to be honest and especially  the laughter there was a part at  Sundance the first time I saw it where  it really felt as if it as if the  audience was laughing at her and I think  they were laughing at her and of course  I relate so deeply to her even though  obviously I note you know she's so  flawed but and I think well what was so  what's so cool about it is they were  laughing at her because they had been so  allied with her and then she really  disturbed them and I think the laughter  in some ways was like I had nothing to  do with this I was never on her side  I like this woman is nothing like me and  then what's so amazing is right after  that moment you have to be alone with  her weeping in a bathroom and feels the  effect of your laughter on a really real  human  so I don't know I guess I'm very proud  of the of the effect it has and also i'm  proud that you know you can like it or  not like it you can empathize with her  or not empathize with her  you can emphasize with her up till this  point and not after you can go with her  on and off all throughout the movie but  i can say that i know that what we're  offering is something truthful and then  the opportunity to decide for yourself  where you come out on it and i think  especially in america where we're just  we're being lied to all the time and  both sides agree on that  you know the far right says all the  media is lies and the left says the  president is lying to you  i think we're a little bit inured to it  and i think people are hungry for  something truthful and for the  opportunity to like come up with their  own position after being offered  something truthful which is what the  movie does so i feel proud of that i  guess it's always gonna be it's gonna  weaken you and your work to try to fit  yourself into some idea of what you  think other people think you're supposed  to be i mean of course we all do that  and and it weakens our work you know but  the strongest work i think comes from  being finding a confidence and finding  the vulnerability to let who you  actually are into your work and I  actually think vanity which of course we  all have family you know so I'm not late  to all of this can be really problematic  in terms of them in terms of work and I  sometimes they see actors it's like it's  like we all respond to what's human we  also respond to beauty of course but we  really I do anyway I see someone whose  body looks real whose face looks real  whose makeup there's a little messed up  or has some wrinkles you know and I  recognize Humanity in it and the work is  stronger so I think to try to erase all  of those things which  you know I guess in some ways considered  flaws I think really does also weaken  the work but the other thing I would say  this is just like this is more of an  acting thing than like a life thing but  I find if I'm ever just only meaning the  literal meaning of the words I'm like  bored and it's not that good what I I  think makes work that I like to watch of  my own and and of other people's is when  you have some other motive you are  saying the words and you mean them but  there's at least one other thing going  on that you're trying to communicate to  the other person you're working with or  that that that is that is important in  the scene and if it's letter if it's  just literal I'm always in trouble  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]
 

